---
title: A logistics dashboard that answers questions from data
org: Personal project
period: 2026
role: Built solo — semantic layer, query compiler, forecasting, statistical guards, dashboard and deployment.
---

A logistics analytics dashboard: KPI tiles and charts on one surface, a natural-language query
interface on the other, demand forecasting with an inventory recommendation, and an
explainability panel behind every number on both. The dataset is 400 mock orders across one
calendar year.

Unlike most of the work on this site, this one is open:

- **Code:** [github.com/mdhyaksa/spaceship-scout](https://github.com/mdhyaksa/spaceship-scout)
- **Live:** [spaceship-scout.m-dhyaksa.workers.dev](https://spaceship-scout.m-dhyaksa.workers.dev/)
  — the shell loads for anyone, but the API sits behind a login, so the dashboard itself needs
  credentials.

## The model writes a plan, not SQL

The obvious way to build a natural-language query interface is to have the model write SQL. It
demos well. It also means the model's output reaches your database, and every failure is a
database failure — a wrong join, an unbounded scan, a metric silently redefined mid-answer.

Here the model emits a **typed query plan** through a single forced tool call: metrics,
dimensions, filters, time range, ordering, limit. Code compiles that plan to SQL. The model never
sees SQL and never produces it, which turns an open-ended generation problem into a constrained
one where the output space is small enough to validate exhaustively.

It is also not an agent loop. Routing is one field on the plan, so the model makes one decision
per question rather than reacting through several turns — the thing that varies most between
models is how many decisions they can chain without drifting, so the design removes the chaining.

## A semantic layer as the only definition

One YAML file defines every metric, dimension, grain, valid grouping and time anchor. Nothing in
the worker or the frontend defines what a metric means. Changing the meaning of "delayed" is an
edit to that file and a rebuild — no prompt changes, no application changes — and there are
conformance tests that fail if a definition leaks into code.

The dashboard tiles are compiled from the same plan format and go through the same validator as
the chat. That is the property worth having: a dashboard and a chat interface that can disagree
about what a number means are two products, and the disagreement surfaces in a meeting rather
than in a test.

Validation is separate from schema conformance, because a structurally valid plan can still be
semantically nonsense — naming a metric that does not exist, grouping by a field that is not a
valid grain, requesting a window outside the data's coverage, or scanning unbounded. Each of
those is a distinct error code with a message that says what was wrong.

One detail that generalises beyond this project: the data ends on a fixed date, so every relative
range — "last month", "last quarter" — resolves against a declared time anchor rather than the
wall clock. Anchored to `now`, every such question would correctly return zero rows and look like
a bug. The anchor is configuration, switchable to live time when the data is live.

## Forecasting that is willing to refuse

Three methods — moving average, linear trend, simple exponential smoothing — selected by
backtest error, with MAPE or MAE chosen according to whether the series has zeros. Prediction
intervals at 80% and 95% come from the residual spread. From the forecast it derives an
inventory recommendation: safety stock from the demand variance over the lead time at a chosen
service level, a reorder point, and an order-up-to quantity.

The guards matter more than the projection, and this is where I think most forecast features go
wrong. With twelve monthly points, every series here trips a low-confidence banner, and a SKU
with too little history is **refused** rather than extrapolated — with a concrete fallback
pointing at an aggregation level that does have enough data. Fitting a trend line through a
handful of sparse points produces a number with a confidence interval wide enough to be
meaningless, and presenting it next to a chart makes it look considered.

The model's only role in any of this is extracting parameters from the question. Every number is
computed.

## The statistical sufficiency check

This is the part I would most want to be asked about.

After a query executes and before any answer text is written, a deterministic check runs over the
result set. It finds the smallest denominator behind any ratio metric, compares it against a
minimum-group floor declared in the semantic layer, and lists the groups that fall below it. When
the answer would rank groups — carrier A beats carrier B on on-time rate — it runs a
two-proportion z-test, or a chi-square test across more than two groups, and records whether the
ranking is statistically significant.

The answer is then only allowed to claim what the check supports. A difference that does not
clear significance is reported as not distinguishable, not as a ranking.

The reason to build this is that the failure it prevents is the normal case, not an edge case. A
dashboard that will happily tell you a carrier is underperforming on the basis of nine orders is
not a neutral tool — somebody acts on it. Thin slices are a property of segmentation itself, so
the problem does not go away with more data; it moves to whichever segment the user drills into
next. Putting the test between execution and the answer is the only place it reliably applies to
both the chat and the tiles.

Every figure on either surface has an explainability panel behind it: the plan that produced it,
the resolved time window, the row counts, the method selected and the checks that ran. The
intent is that a number should never be the end of the trail.

## Engineering notes

- **Tests** across the compiler, the validator, time resolution, the forecast selection and
  guards, the sufficiency statistics, golden-output snapshots, and a dialect parity suite that
  checks the SQLite and Postgres emitters agree.
- **Portable SQL by construction.** The semantic layer uses plain column references and one
  portable date function that the compiler maps per dialect, so no dialect-specific SQL appears
  in a metric definition. The parity tests are what keep that true.
- **Secrets never reach the client.** The API key lives as a deployment secret; the browser
  bundle carries no metric definitions, thresholds or glossary, because those stay server-side.
  The auth gate fails closed — with no credentials configured, nothing authenticates, because the
  opposite default turns one forgotten deployment step into a public dataset.
- **Degrades honestly.** The dashboard, forecasts and every explainability panel work with no
  model access at all, since they are hand-written plans. Only the chat needs a key, and it says
  so rather than failing opaquely.

## Stack

TypeScript throughout. React and Recharts on the client, Cloudflare Workers and D1 on the server,
Zod for schema validation, Vitest for tests, deployed from the repository with Wrangler.
